2026-09-16T05:54Z PLATFORM HALF SETTLED, and it is the bad answer: no intervention on a stalled extraction is possible from this side. Read against the shipped Zotero 10.0.2 on this machine (~/.local/Zotero_linux-x86_64/app/omni.ja, BuildID 20260909184950) rather than from documentation or memory. ensure()'s whole option surface is isPriority and onProgress; setTimeout, Promise.race, AbortController and AbortSignal appear zero times in sdt.js and pdfWorker/manager.js; the worker dispatcher accepts twelve actions and none cancels. The isPriority caveat this ticket already carried is confirmed at the source: _processQueue shifts the running item off _queue before awaiting, so priority reorders only jobs not yet started. Structurally worse than a missing parameter: extraction runs in the worker's single self.onmessage and the block-seg ONNX models run as WASM on that same thread, so a cancel message could not be dequeued by the thread that would act on it. Zotero.PDFWorker._worker.terminate() IS reachable from a plugin sandbox and MUST NOT be used: the parked promise settles only from the message listener, so it never settles, _processingQueue never returns to false, and the shared worker queue -- full-text indexing, annotation import, recognizer -- never drains again for the life of the process. One stuck job becomes a stuck queue. The cache cannot be corrupted; the pack is written atomically in the parent after the bytes return. No watchdog exists in the worker path: fulltext.js's stall detector watches database queue counts and its maxTime is a slice budget checked between items, never evaluated when the worker hangs under the await. The decisive evidence that this is an omission and not a design: upstream wrote the worker recycling at the right hook point in manager.js and shipped it commented out. Consequence for this ticket: detection is supported and intervention is not, so the corrected exit criteria -- announce, never bound -- are right for a reason stronger than the one recorded when they were corrected. Recorded in DESIGN-NOTES.md open question 4, whose platform half this closes. NOT filed upstream: that is outward-facing and the author's to send, and this ticket's own run decides whether the case is a wedge at all.
