--- body ---
## Context

The 2026-09-15T05:22Z sweep of the author's full library (sitter 0.4.15,
Zotero 10.0.2, pdf processor 14) stopped advancing on one document and was
ended by hand. The document is the IPCC AR6 WG1 report: **3 949 pages,
242 MB**, born digital, `itemID` 11940 / key `AI6DJUTA`.

What the trace shows, and only what it shows:

- progress ran **49 → 90** in 126 s, one tick every 2–3 s;
- then **90 held for at least 173 s**, phase still `extracting`, across three
  60 s heartbeats. The extension is alive throughout — the heartbeats prove it;
- two `switch enabled:false` records 11 s apart, then `shutdown reason:uninstall`.
- `storage/AI6DJUTA/` holds a `.zotero-ft-cache` and **no `.zotero-sdt-cache`**:
  no pack was persisted.

Nothing crashed. No `.dmp`, no `crashes/events` entry, no kernel OOM — and that
last zero is a read, not a refusal: `journalctl -k` returns 101 lines over the
same window.

The sitter does not extract. It calls `Zotero.SDT.ensure(id, {isPriority:false,
onProgress})` (`plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js:3433`) and the number it reports
is the native worker's own. `scheduler.js:357-368` documents that once handed to
the native worker the call cannot be told to stop — which is why the switch had
to be thrown twice.

**The open question, and the whole of this ticket:** at the observed rate the
remaining ~10 % (≈ 395 pages) should have taken ~25 s, not ≥ 173 s. Is the
native worker still advancing — coarse progress granularity, or a genuinely slow
tail — or is it wedged? Both answers are actionable and they point at different
fixes. Neither is established, and `scheduler.js:376-381` already warns that
from the sitter's side "a worker that ran out of memory and a photograph that
holds no text are indistinguishable".

Guard 2 of 0727 is unbuilt for exactly this reason: a check that cannot fire
must not ship. Same rule here — no sitter change lands off this trace alone.

**The question is also open in the project's own ledger.**
`plugins/sdt-sitter/DESIGN-NOTES.md`, open question 4, asks "Is submitted
extraction meant to become cancellable?" — the same question wearing a different
hat. That entry now cites this ticket and records why it stays open.

## What is built, and what is still owed

The instrument landed ahead of the run, because a measurement taken with an
unvalidated instrument is not a measurement:

- `bench/sdt_stall_probe.py` — the plateau predicate over heartbeat records, a
  per-thread `/proc` CPU/RSS/IO sampler, and `stall_verdict()`, the one place
  the two meet and the only place a cause is named.
- `tests/test_sdt_stall_probe.py` — both control arms of the predicate, asserted
  through one call with one set of thresholds, and the sampler's own two
  controls (a synthetic busy loop and a sleeping process).
- `verification/incidents/0793-2026-09-15-stall-probe-controls.md` and
  `…-ar6-stall-reading.json` — the instrument applied to the preserved trace.
  Its verdict today is **undetermined**: the plateau is confirmed, and with no
  CPU sample beside it a slow tail and a wedge are indistinguishable. That
  undetermined is why this ticket stays open.

Still owed: the AR6 run itself. Only the author can make it — see below.

## The single experiment

Start Zotero on `~/data/Zotero-fresh` with the sitter installed and the AR6
(`storage/AI6DJUTA/`, 3 949 pages, 242 MB) in the queue, note its PID, and from a
second terminal run
`python3 bench/sdt_stall_probe.py --pid <PID> --seconds 2400 --interval 5
--version 0.4.20 --zotero 10.0.2 --json-out verification/incidents/0793-<date>-ar6-run.json`,
then leave the window alone — do **not** throw the switch, do **not** uninstall
— until the sampler exits, which is at least thirty minutes past the moment the
progress bar stops moving. Quit Zotero normally afterwards, copy
`~/data/Zotero-fresh/sdt-sitter-last-shutdown.json` (it is overwritten by the
next shutdown) beside the run, and re-run the probe with `--journal` pointed at
that copy so the plateau reading and the CPU series sit in one record. **The
reading that decides it is the busiest thread's percent across the beats where
progress is frozen**: with the heartbeats still firing — which proves the
extension is alive and the sampler is looking at a live process — a worker
thread holding **≥ 10 % of a core** through the plateau means the native worker
is still working and the plateau is a *slow tail*; a worker thread at **≤ 2 %**
through the plateau means it has stopped and the document is *wedged*. Anything
between those bands the probe reports as undetermined rather than rounding to
the answer one was hoping for; if that happens, sample longer rather than
narrowing the bands. Also record, at the end, whether progress ever ticked past
90 and whether `storage/AI6DJUTA/.zotero-sdt-cache` exists — both are one-line
facts that the journal cannot supply and that decide whether a pack was ever
persisted.

**Three further readings the probe takes before it will say *wedged*,** each of
which is a flat worker thread that is nonetheless not a wedge. Read them off
the record rather than re-deriving them: (1) the **whole process** must also be
under 10 % of a core *averaged over the window* — thread identification is a
heuristic (busiest thread) and a rotation inside the window defeats it, so a
busy process with a flat "worker" is reported as undetermined; (2) the process
must have read **under 1 MB**, counting both `/proc/<pid>/io`'s `read_bytes`
(block layer) and `rchar` (syscall layer, which is the one that moves when the
pages are already in cache) — a worker blocked on I/O holds ~0 % CPU and is
working; (3) those counters must have been **readable at all** — where
`/proc/<pid>/io` is unavailable the probe refuses a wedge rather than granting
one on a channel nobody consulted.

**A second limit, and this run is what settles it.** Reading (2) is
*process-wide* and the recipe samples for 2 400 s. An idle Python interpreter
accrued 52 517 bytes of `rchar` in 3 s from startup alone — about 5 % of the
1 MB floor — so over forty minutes a live Zotero's ambient housekeeping may
clear that floor whatever the worker is doing, which would make `wedged`
unreachable. It has not been tuned on no data, because it fails toward
`undetermined` rather than toward a false verdict. The record carries the
**worker thread's own** `read_bytes` and `rchar` beside the process-wide
figures precisely so one run answers it: if the process cleared the floor while
the worker thread read nothing, the floor should be thread-scoped, and that is
a one-line change once there is a measurement behind it.

One further known limit, stated so it is not discovered from the run: the verdict is
decided on the **window average**, not on peaks, because over 480 five-second
intervals of a live desktop application one unrelated CPU burst is a
near-certainty and a peak-based veto would make `wedged` almost unreachable.
The peaks and the count of busy intervals are recorded in the verdict's own
`why` string. If they show a *burst* pattern — a worker idle on average but
repeatedly busy for one interval at a time — that is neither of the two
readings this ticket offers, and it is a finding in its own right.

`johnson-1785-dictionary` (1 104 pages, 124 MB, already in the fixture cache) is
worth running in the same session as a pipeline-realism control: it crosses the
page cap, it exercises the same path at a comparable size, and it tells us how
long a document that *does* settle sits on its last tick. It **cannot** prove
the predicate detects a wedge, because Johnson always finishes — a green Johnson
run establishes only that the instrument sees a completion. Nobody should read
one as sufficient. The wedge control that Johnson cannot be is filed as ticket
0799 (a mock `host.ensure` that never resolves, at the scheduler level).

## Relevant files

- `verification/incidents/0793-2026-09-15-ar6-stall.json` — the preserved trace,
